      "heading": "Clinical Evidence: What Does Research Say About this diet for Blood Sugar?",
      "body": "<p>Clinical trials that evaluate structured meal planning for restaurant visits report small but meaningful reductions in HbA1c and fasting glucose. A randomized controlled trial found a 0.3% decrease in HbA1c after 12 weeks of guided meal selection, while a meta‑analysis of 10 studies reported a mean fasting glucose drop of 8 mg/dL (0.5 mmol/L) (Gal N, 2020; Sun J & Wang G, 2024). These outcomes were achieved without significant weight loss, suggesting the benefit is driven by carbohydrate quality and portion control rather than calorie restriction. The evidence base is primarily composed of RCTs and systematic reviews, providing moderate confidence in the effectiveness of this dietary pattern for blood sugar management. However, most studies were short‑term (≤12 weeks) and involved relatively small sample sizes, indicating a need for longer‑term research.</p>\n",
